"""
Something that permissions attach to, and that can be recorded as having acted on an Admin
resource.

A principal is a human (`AdminUser`), a collection of humans (`AdminGroup`), or a non-human
identity such as an app, service, or agent. Further kinds are added as further implementations
of this interface rather than as a discriminator field on a single type, so that `__typename`
reports which kind was returned and callers select the fields that exist for that kind.

This is a cross-domain interface. Any Admin object that records "who did this" or "who holds
this" should reference it rather than declaring its own actor shape.

Implementations are the canonical entity types, not denormalized copies of them, which is the
point of modelling this as an interface: the `AdminUser` behind `AdminOAuthClient.createdBy` is
the same object, with the same ID, as the one `admin_user` returns. That keeps a single cache
record per account and lets a credential's creator be composed with the queries that search
accounts, instead of being a parallel half-populated shape.

A field of this type is a resolved reference and is nullable, because the account it points at
may no longer exist. Objects that reference a principal should also expose the underlying
account ID as a plain scalar - see `AdminOAuthClient.createdByAccountId` - so that closing an
account does not erase the audit trail. There is deliberately no "unknown principal"
implementation: an unresolvable account is missing data, not a kind of principal, and modelling
it as a type would make every caller branch on `__typename` to discover that.

The interface is deliberately narrow. Fields that only some kinds of principal carry - email
address, account status, organization membership, directory attributes - belong on the
implementations, and can be promoted here later if every implementation turns out to carry
them.

Not every principal can authenticate. `AdminGroup` can hold a role, but it cannot own a
credential or appear as the actor in an audit trail, so a field that always resolves to
something that acts still type-admits a group. Such fields document what they actually return;
the interface is not the constraint.
"""
interface AdminPrincipal {
  "Unique ID of the principal."
  id: ID!
  "Display name of the principal."
  name: String
}

